      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because product managers can't plan, but because we ask roadmaps to do the impossible. We want them to inspire vision, align executives, satisfy sales, promise customers certainty, and guide engineers — all at once. Stretched across these conflicting jobs, the roadmap snaps. It stops being a tool for strategy and becomes theatre: a pretty timeline that everyone nods at and nobody trusts.\n\nFirst, most roadmaps confuse outputs with outcomes. They list features and dates — \"mobile app in Q2, AI chatbot in Q3\" — instead of the problems to solve or the impact to create. This turns product into a feature factory. Teams are measured on shipping on time, not on whether anything got better for users or the business. When a date slips, or a feature flops, the roadmap looks like a failure, even if the team learned something valuable.\n\nSecond, they lack strategy. A great roadmap should be a visual argument: this is where we're playing, this is how we'll win, and therefore this is what matters now. Too many are just aggregated wish lists — a little for Sales, a little for the CEO, a little for the biggest customer. Without ruthless prioritization and explicit trade-offs, everything is P0, which means nothing is. The roadmap tries to keep peace rather than make choices, and so it pleases no one for long.\n\nThird, they pretend uncertainty doesn't exist. Products live in a world of shifting customers, competitors, and technical surprises. Yet roadmaps are often carved in stone for 12-18 months, built on untested assumptions and no discovery. There's no room to learn, pivot, or kill bad ideas. Teams either follow the plan blindly off a cliff, or constantly replan in panic. In both cases, trust erodes.\n\nThe roadmaps that succeed do the opposite. They start with outcomes — reduce churn by 10%, expand into mid-market — and hold dates loosely and learning tightly. They make strategy explicit, show what they're *not* doing, and get updated as evidence comes in. Most importantly, they're treated as communication tools for alignment, not contracts for delivery.\n\nA roadmap doesn't fail when a date changes. It fails when it stops helping a team decide what matters next.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they are built as static forecasts in a dynamic environment. Companies treat roadmaps as promises to customers, investors, and internal stakeholders, then try to defend them against reality. When user behavior, competition, or costs change, the roadmap becomes a source of tension rather than a tool for decision-making.\n\nThe first reason is that roadmaps often start from solutions instead of problems. Teams list features—dashboards, integrations, AI assistants—without articulating the customer pains those features should solve. This creates a roadmap that looks concrete but has weak foundations. If the problem statement is wrong, every downstream commitment inherits the error.\n\nSecond, many roadmaps ignore uncertainty. Product work is inherently ambiguous: requirements shift, experiments fail, and dependencies surface late. Yet roadmaps are frequently presented with false precision, showing dates and outcomes as if the future were known. This forces teams to guess, inflate estimates, or deprioritize discovery work that could reveal a better path. The result is a plan that is confidently wrong rather than adaptively useful.\n\nThird, roadmaps fail because they assume a stable organization. In reality, priorities change with strategy, budgets, leadership, and market pressure. When executives or sales teams introduce new demands, the roadmap becomes a battleground for competing interests. Without a clear mechanism for saying no or reprioritizing, it turns into a wish list that nobody trusts.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps are communication artifacts, not management systems. Teams publish them once and then stop referring to them. They are not reviewed against leading indicators, customer feedback, or delivery performance. Because learning is not built into the roadmap’s cadence, it cannot guide better decisions over time.\n\nA useful roadmap is not a commitment calendar but a hypothesis map. It should show outcomes, bets, evidence, and the conditions under which plans will change. It should be revised often and framed as a living decision tool. Product teams that treat uncertainty as normal and focus on problems, not features, are far less likely to watch their roadmaps quietly fail.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
